   295	
   296	   If the input has no unpairable lines, a GNU extension is available;
   297	the sort order can be any order that considers two fields to be equal if
   298	and only if the sort comparison described above considers them to be
   299	equal.  For example:
   300	
   301	     $ cat file1
   302	     a a1
   303	     c c1
   304	     b b1
   305	
   306	     $ cat file2
   307	     a a2
   308	     c c2
   309	     b b2
   310	
   311	     $ join file1 file2
   312	     a a1 a2
   313	     c c1 c2
   314	     b b1 b2
   315	
   316	8.3.5 Header lines
   317	------------------
   318	
   319	The ‘--header’ option can be used when the files to join have a header
   320	line which is not sorted:
   321	
   322	     $ cat file1
   323	     Name     Age
   324	     Alice    25
   325	     Charlie  34
   326	
   327	     $ cat file2
   328	     Name   Country
   329	     Alice  France
   330	     Bob    Spain
   331	
   332	     $ join --header -o auto -e NA -a1 -a2 file1 file2
   333	     Name     Age   Country
   334	     Alice    25    France
   335	     Bob      NA    Spain
   336	     Charlie  34    NA
   337	
   338	   To sort a file with a header line, use GNU ‘sed -u’.  The following
   339	example sort the files but keeps the first line of each file in place:
   340	
   341	     $ ( sed -u 1q ; sort -k2b,2 ) < file1 > file1.sorted
   342	     $ ( sed -u 1q ; sort -k2b,2 ) < file2 > file2.sorted
   343	     $ join --header -o auto -e NA -a1 -a2 file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3
   344	
   345	8.3.6 Union, Intersection and Difference of files
   346	-------------------------------------------------
   347	
   348	Combine ‘sort’, ‘uniq’ and ‘join’ to perform the equivalent of set
   349	operations on files:
   350	
   351	Command                              outcome
   352	--------------------------------------------------------------------------
   353	‘sort -u file1 file2’                Union of unsorted files
   354	                                     
   355	‘sort file1 file2 | uniq -d’         Intersection of unsorted files
   356	                                     
   357	‘sort file1 file1 file2 | uniq -u’   Difference of unsorted files
   358	                                     
   359	‘sort file1 file2 | uniq -u’         Symmetric Difference of unsorted
   360	                                     files
   361	                                     
   362	‘join -t '' -a1 -a2 file1 file2’     Union of sorted files
   363	                                     
   364	‘join -t '' file1 file2’             Intersection of sorted files
   365	                                     
   366	‘join -t '' -v2 file1 file2’         Difference of sorted files
   367	                                     
   368	‘join -t '' -v1 -v2 file1 file2’     Symmetric Difference of sorted
   369	                                     files
   370	                                     
   371	
   372	   All examples above operate on entire lines and not on specific
   373	fields: ‘sort’ without ‘-k’ and ‘join -t ''’ both consider entire lines
   374	as the key.
   375	
   376	2 Common options
   377	****************
   378	
   379	Certain options are available in all of these programs.  Rather than
   380	writing identical descriptions for each of the programs, they are
   381	described here.  (In fact, every GNU program accepts (or should accept)
   382	these options.)
   383	
   384	   Normally options and operands can appear in any order, and programs
   385	act as if all the options appear before any operands.  For example,
   386	‘sort -r passwd -t :’ acts like ‘sort -r -t : passwd’, since ‘:’ is an
   387	option-argument of ‘-t’.  However, if the ‘POSIXLY_CORRECT’ environment
   388	variable is set, options must appear before operands, unless otherwise
   389	specified for a particular command.
   390	
   391	   A few programs can usefully have trailing operands with leading ‘-’.
   392	With such a program, options must precede operands even if
   393	‘POSIXLY_CORRECT’ is not set, and this fact is noted in the program
   394	description.  For example, the ‘env’ command’s options must appear
   395	before its operands, since in some cases the operands specify a command
   396	that itself contains options.
   397	
   398	   Most programs that accept long options recognize unambiguous
   399	abbreviations of those options.  For example, ‘rmdir
   400	--ignore-fail-on-non-empty’ can be invoked as ‘rmdir --ignore-fail’ or
   401	even ‘rmdir --i’.  Ambiguous options, such as ‘ls --h’, are identified
   402	as such.
   403	
   404	   Some of these programs recognize the ‘--help’ and ‘--version’ options
   405	only when one of them is the sole command line argument.  For these
   406	programs, abbreviations of the long options are not always recognized.
   407	
   408	‘--help’
   409	     Print a usage message listing all available options, then exit
   410	     successfully.
   411	
   412	‘--version’
   413	     Print the version number, then exit successfully.
   414	
   415	‘--’
   416	     Delimit the option list.  Later arguments, if any, are treated as
   417	     operands even if they begin with ‘-’.  For example, ‘sort -- -r’
   418	     reads from the file named ‘-r’.
   419	
   420	   A single ‘-’ operand is not really an option, though it looks like
   421	one.  It stands for a file operand, and some tools treat it as standard
   422	input, or as standard output if that is clear from the context.  For
   423	example, ‘sort -’ reads from standard input, and is equivalent to plain
   424	‘sort’.  Unless otherwise specified, a ‘-’ can appear as any operand
   425	that requires a file name.
   426	
   427	* Menu:
   428	
   429	* Exit status::                 Indicating program success or failure.
   430	* Backup options::              -b -S, in some programs.
   431	* Block size::                  BLOCK_SIZE and –block-size, in some programs.
   432	* Floating point::              Floating point number representation.
   433	* Signal specifications::       Specifying signals using the –signal option.
   434	* Disambiguating names and IDs:: chgrp, chown, chroot, id: user and group syntax
   435	* Random sources::              –random-source, in some programs.
   436	* Target directory::            Specifying a target directory, in some programs.
   437	* Trailing slashes::            –strip-trailing-slashes, in some programs.
   438	* Traversing symlinks::         -H, -L, or -P, in some programs.
   439	* Treating / specially::        –preserve-root and –no-preserve-root.
   440	* Special built-in utilities::  ‘break’, ‘:’, ...
   441	* Standards conformance::       Conformance to the POSIX standard.
   442	* Multi-call invocation::       Multi-call program invocation.
   443	
   444	2.1 Exit status
   445	===============
   446	
   447	Nearly every command invocation yields an integral “exit status” that
   448	can be used to change how other commands work.  For the vast majority of
   449	commands, an exit status of zero indicates success.  Failure is
   450	indicated by a nonzero value—typically ‘1’, though it may differ on
   451	unusual platforms as POSIX requires only that it be nonzero.
   452	
   453	   However, some of the programs documented here do produce other exit
   454	status values and a few associate different meanings with the values ‘0’
   455	and ‘1’.  Here are some of the exceptions: ‘chroot’, ‘env’, ‘expr’,
   456	‘nice’, ‘nohup’, ‘numfmt’, ‘printenv’, ‘sort’, ‘stdbuf’, ‘test’,
   457	‘timeout’, ‘tty’.
[?2004hroot@e1cc0dde70ad:/app# printf '\n--- GNU reference ---\n'
[?2004l
--- GNU reference ---
[?2004hroot@e1cc0dde70ad:/app# join --version | head -1
[?2004lbash: join: command not found
[?2004hroot@e1cc0dde70ad:/app# sed -n '88,230p' /app/docs/join.txt
[?2004l‘-o FIELD-LIST’
‘-o auto’
     If the keyword ‘auto’ is specified, infer the output format from
     the first line in each file.  This is the same as the default
     output format but also ensures the same number of fields are output
     for each line.  Missing fields are replaced with the ‘-e’ option
     and extra fields are discarded.

     Otherwise, construct each output line according to the format in
     FIELD-LIST.  Each element in FIELD-LIST is either the single
     character ‘0’ or has the form M.N where the file number, M, is ‘1’
     or ‘2’ and N is a positive field number.

     A field specification of ‘0’ denotes the join field.  In most
     cases, the functionality of the ‘0’ field spec may be reproduced
     using the explicit M.N that corresponds to the join field.
     However, when printing unpairable lines (using either of the ‘-a’
     or ‘-v’ options), there is no way to specify the join field using
     M.N in FIELD-LIST if there are unpairable lines in both files.  To
     give ‘join’ that functionality, POSIX invented the ‘0’ field
     specification notation.

     The elements in FIELD-LIST are separated by commas or blanks.
     Blank separators typically need to be quoted for the shell.  For
     example, the commands ‘join -o 1.2,2.2’ and ‘join -o '1.2 2.2'’ are
     equivalent.

     All output lines—including those printed because of any -a or -v
     option—are subject to the specified FIELD-LIST.

‘-t CHAR’
     Use character CHAR as the input and output field separator.  Treat
     as significant each occurrence of CHAR in the input file.  Use
     ‘sort -t CHAR’, without the ‘-b’ option of ‘sort’, to produce this
     ordering.  If ‘join -t ''’ is specified, the whole line is
     considered, matching the default operation of sort.  If ‘-t '\0'’
     is specified then the ASCII NUL character is used to delimit the
     fields.

‘-v FILE-NUMBER’
     Print a line for each unpairable line in file FILE-NUMBER (either
     ‘1’ or ‘2’), instead of the normal output.

‘-z’
‘--zero-terminated’
     Delimit items with a zero byte rather than a newline (ASCII LF).
     I.e., treat input as items separated by ASCII NUL and terminate
     output items with ASCII NUL. This option can be useful in
     conjunction with ‘perl -0’ or ‘find -print0’ and ‘xargs -0’ which
     do the same in order to reliably handle arbitrary file names (even
     those containing blanks or other special characters).  Note with
     ‘-z’ the newline character is treated as a field separator.

   An exit status of zero indicates success, and a nonzero value
indicates failure.

   If the ‘--check-order’ option is given, unsorted inputs will cause a
fatal error message.  If the option ‘--nocheck-order’ is given, unsorted
inputs will never cause an error message.  If neither of these options
is given, wrongly sorted inputs are diagnosed only if an input file is
found to contain unpairable lines, and when both input files are non
empty.  If an input file is diagnosed as being unsorted, the ‘join’
command will exit with a nonzero status (and the output should not be
used).

   Forcing ‘join’ to process wrongly sorted input files containing
unpairable lines by specifying ‘--nocheck-order’ is not guaranteed to
produce any particular output.  The output will probably not correspond
with whatever you hoped it would be.

8.3.2 Pre-sorting
-----------------

‘join’ requires sorted input files.  Each input file should be sorted
according to the key (=field/column number) used in ‘join’.  The
recommended sorting option is ‘sort -k 1b,1’ (assuming the desired key
is in the first column).

Typical usage:
     $ sort -k 1b,1 file1 > file1.sorted
     $ sort -k 1b,1 file2 > file2.sorted
     $ join file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3

   Normally, the sort order is that of the collating sequence specified
by the ‘LC_COLLATE’ locale.  Unless the ‘-t’ option is given, the sort
comparison ignores blanks at the start of the join field, as in ‘sort
-b’.  If the ‘--ignore-case’ option is given, the sort comparison
ignores the case of characters in the join field, as in ‘sort -f’:

     $ sort -k 1bf,1 file1 > file1.sorted
     $ sort -k 1bf,1 file2 > file2.sorted
     $ join --ignore-case file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3

   The ‘sort’ and ‘join’ commands should use consistent locales and
options if the output of ‘sort’ is fed to ‘join’.  You can use a command
like ‘sort -k 1b,1’ to sort a file on its default join field, but if you
select a non-default locale, join field, separator, or comparison
options, then you should do so consistently between ‘join’ and ‘sort’.

To avoid any locale-related issues, it is recommended to use the ‘C’
locale for both commands:

     $ LC_ALL=C sort -k 1b,1 file1 > file1.sorted
     $ LC_ALL=C sort -k 1b,1 file2 > file2.sorted
     $ LC_ALL=C join file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3

8.3.3 Working with fields
-------------------------

Use ‘-1’,‘-2’ to set the key fields for each of the input files.  Ensure
the preceding ‘sort’ commands operated on the same fields.

The following example joins two files, using the values from seventh
field of the first file and the third field of the second file:

     $ sort -k 7b,7 file1 > file1.sorted
     $ sort -k 3b,3 file2 > file2.sorted
     $ join -1 7 -2 3 file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3

If the field number is the same for both files, use ‘-j’:

     $ sort -k4b,4 file1 > file1.sorted
     $ sort -k4b,4 file2 > file2.sorted
     $ join -j4    file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3

Both ‘sort’ and ‘join’ operate of whitespace-delimited fields.  To
specify a different delimiter, use ‘-t’ in _both_:

     $ sort -t, -k3b,3 file1 > file1.sorted
     $ sort -t, -k3b,3 file2 > file2.sorted
     $ join -t, -j3    file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3

To specify a tab (ASCII 0x09) character instead of whitespace, use (1):

     $ sort -t$'\t' -k3b,3 file1 > file1.sorted
     $ sort -t$'\t' -k3b,3 file2 > file2.sorted
     $ join -t$'\t' -j3    file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3

If ‘join -t ''’ is specified then the whole line is considered which
matches the default operation of sort:

     $ sort file1 > file1.sorted
     $ sort file2 > file2.sorted
